From c84bad212e54c22024bfde53b04916d026563423 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 08:29:29 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08110/11362] [outer-loop] ARC: goal-predicate shadowing
 measured -- mechanism confirmed, claimed benefit refuted

The split-induce fallback concatenates an engine-only completion with a goal-only
one. The model routinely writes an is_level_complete in BOTH, and Python binds the
second -- the one generated from a prompt carrying no grid and no transitions.

MECHANISM: CONFIRMED. Within one corpus, 23/23 files carrying _combine_world_model's
concatenation signature define the function twice vs 0/93 that do not (Fisher
p=2.3e-14); a separate corpus of 40 raw single-call completions has 0. The shipped
resolver _find_function picks the second definition in 23/23.

CLAIMED BENEFIT: NOT CONFIRMED, and this is the headline. Grading BOTH definitions
of all 23 cells through the shipped goal gate -- same engine, same root grid, one
killable subprocess each -- gives shadowed 2 satisfiable vs bound 1, with 20 of 23
cells tied at unsatisfiable (exact McNemar p=1.0). The hypothesis that a good
predicate is being thrown away is not supported by the data.

A one-sided VALIDITY difference exists but is underpowered: 4 of 23 bound
definitions are not usable predicates at all (2 return None, 1 raises NameError,
1 genuinely non-terminating -- re-tested in isolation, a while-loop that never
pops) vs 0 of 23 shadowed. Only 4 discordant pairs, so exact p=0.125.

Robustness: on the 3 games with a recorded planner root, that root is byte-identical
to the arcade opening board and 0/10 arm verdicts flip. Both sweeps were run twice
end to end; every verdict and p-value reproduced, only wall-clock moved.

FIX, behind CARNOT_ARC_GOAL_DEDUP, DEFAULT OFF, justified as eliminating a defect by
construction and saving one generation call -- NOT by a measured quality gain:
  - skip the goal-only generation when the engine half already supplied a valid,
    non-declined predicate, so no second definition is ever created;
  - excise the engine half's definition in _combine_world_model otherwise, so the
    file defines the function exactly once whichever way it got there.
Binding order is deliberately NOT reordered: the shipped static validator returns the
last top-level definition, and disagreeing with it would be worse than the defect.
The dedup write re-prepends `import numpy as np` because the join guaranteed it.

Inertness with the flag off is proved mechanically (prove_inertness.py): AST diff vs
HEAD shows no new executable statement outside a guard, a least-fixed-point over the
call graph (modelling `and` short-circuit) shows all four new helpers unreachable,
and the flag resolves as value.strip()=='1' like every sibling. 7 artifacts pinning
this module get freshness acknowledgements citing that proof rather than a rebuild,
which would have destroyed their measured timings.

22 tests. Against the pre-fix module recovered via `git show HEAD:`, 15 fail and 2
pass -- the 2 being the binding-order pin and the flag-off byte-identical control.
578 pass across the ARC goal/world-model/induce suites. Three pre-existing failures
(trust_energy 4494, 4537, 4821) fail identically at HEAD and are not from this change.

